# Internationalization (i18n) rules

## Translation structure

- **Organize by resource** - translations are organized by resource (e.g. `vacancies/strings`, `home/strings`)
- **Translation files** - each resource has files for each language: `strings.ru.json`, `strings.en.json`, `strings.de.json`, etc.
- **Menu** - separate files for menu: `menu.ru.json`, `menu.en.json`, etc.

## Translation synchronization

- **`strings.ru.json` - source of truth** - the `strings.ru.json` file is the primary source for all translations
- **Sync all languages** - when changing translations, update all language files (`strings.*.json`) in each i18n subfolder (root, `saas`, `sap`, etc.)
- **Preserve structure** - all language files must have the same structure and the same number of keys

## Using TranslationService

- **Load resources** - use `TranslationService.loadResource$()` to load translations
- **`isResourceLoaded` pattern** - use the `isResourceLoaded` flag to control loading
- **Caching** - `TranslationService` automatically caches loaded translations

Example:
```typescript
export class VacanciesComponent implements OnInit {
  isResourceLoaded = false;
  protected readonly resource = 'vacancies/strings';

  constructor(
    private readonly translationService: TranslationService,
    private readonly destroyRef: DestroyRef,
  ) {
  }

  ngOnInit(): void {
    if (this.translationService.isResourceLoaded(this.resource)) {
      this.isResourceLoaded = true;
    } else {
      this.translationService.loadResource$(this.resource)
        .pipe(
          finalize(() => this.isResourceLoaded = true),
          takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef),
        )
        .subscribe();
    }
  }
}
```

## Using TranslatePipe

- **In templates** - use `TranslatePipe` to translate text in templates
- **Specify resource** - specify the resource via the `:resource` parameter
- **REQUIRED for all user-facing text** - ALL user-visible text must use translations via the `translate` pipe
- **Direct string values forbidden** - do NOT use direct string values for `label`, `placeholder`, headings, descriptions, and other user-facing text

Example:
```html
<!-- ✅ Correct -->
<h1>{{ 'row_15_title' | translate:resource }}</h1>
<p>{{ 'row_15_description' | translate:resource }}</p>
<app-input-text
  label="{{ 'common_name' | translate }}"
  placeholder="{{ 'common_name_placeholder' | translate }}"
/>

<!-- ❌ INCORRECT -->
<h1>Personal Information</h1>
<p>Our Talent Acquisition team will contact you</p>
<app-input-text
  label="Your Country of Residence"
  placeholder="Please enter the country you are legally registered to work in"
/>
```

**CRITICAL**: All text in forms, headings, descriptions, labels, and placeholders MUST use translation keys via `{{ 'key' | translate }}`. Direct string values in English or any other language are FORBIDDEN.

## Getting translations in components

- **`translationService.get()`** - use to get translations in TypeScript code
- **Specify resource** - always specify the resource as the second parameter

Example:
```typescript
const title = this.translationService.get('row_17_title', this.resource);
const description = this.translationService.get('row_17_description', this.resource);
```

## Translation keys

- **Key naming** - use a component/section prefix (e.g. `row_15_title`, `row_16_description`)
- **Snake case** - use snake_case for translation keys
- **Descriptive names** - keys should be clear and describe the context

Example structure:
```json
{
  "row_15_title": "Careers",
  "row_15_description": "Join our team",
  "row_16_title": "Open positions",
  "row_17_title": "Submit resume"
}
```

## Common keys

- **`common_*`** - use the `common_` prefix for shared translations (e.g. `common_search`, `common_filter_by`)
- **Store in root** - store common translations in `strings.*.json` at the root of `i18n/`

Example:
```json
{
  "common_search": "Search",
  "common_filter_by": "Filter by",
  "common_phone_number": "Phone number",
  "common_attach_resume": "Attach resume"
}
```

## JSON formatting

- **2-space indent** - use 2 spaces for indentation in JSON files
- **Trailing comma** - trailing comma is allowed in translation JSON files
- **Single-line objects** - translation objects on one line (if not too long)
